APCOMP 297r Intellectual Property Information
Section 1. Course Policy on Intellectual Property & Open Science Baseline
Students and industry partners acknowledge that the success of academic scholarship requires the freedom of ideas, and agree to classify data or code as "confidential" or "proprietary" only to the minimal extent necessary to comply with bona fide business requirements such as contractual agreements and applicable law.
Baseline Course IP is Public Open Source: Unless an alternative proprietary track is explicitly designated and agreed upon in writing prior to project work, student teams and industry partners agree to make all software and code generated in the Course public under a permissive open source license, such as the MIT License or Apache 2.0. All documentation, written reports, and non-code assets shall be made available under a Creative Commons Attribution license (CC-BY 4.0). Under this baseline, no party claims exclusive proprietary rights, and students, partners, and the general public enjoy perpetual, royalty-free, worldwide rights to use, modify, and distribute the work.
Industry partners retain exclusive ownership of any pre-existing data and derivative data ("Partner Data"), and code ("Partner Code") contributed to Course activities, unless a more permissive license has been or is expressly granted. Students shall have the right, at no cost, to the most permissive license to Partner Data and/or Partner Code available to the general public.
Students agree not to disclose Partner Data or Partner Code to any other party, including students working on other projects in the Course, without the consent of the applicable partner unless otherwise expressly permitted by consent or license terms. If the partner requests a reasonable nondisclosure agreement (NDA) governing Partner Data or Partner Code, students agree to either (a) execute such an agreement or (b) request reassignment to a different project without an NDA, in which case course staff shall honor the reassignment request.
Students agree to make all commercially reasonable efforts to protect Partner Data, Partner Code, and Course deliverables, including (a) storing it in a manner as required by the applicable partner, in any case consistent with industry standard practices for information security, and (b) deleting any and all Partner Data and Partner Code or online access thereto at the end of the Course, unless otherwise permitted by the applicable partner (for example, as part of the Enabling License defined below).
Alternative Proprietary Arrangements: Where a partner cannot operate under the baseline Open Science framework and requires confidential data handling, the project may operate under a Joint IP framework. In such cases, student teams and industry partners jointly own the intellectual property produced in the Course (the "Course IP"). To avoid doubt, Course IP does not include Partner Data or Partner Code. In this track, industry partners and each student contributing to the Course IP receive a nonexclusive, perpetual, royalty-free, worldwide license to the Course IP (the "Limited License"). The Limited License is not transferable unless the industry partner (or substantially all of its assets) is acquired, in which case the Limited License is transferred to the partner's successor or assignee. This Limited License has been adopted as course policy as a material inducement to partners who participate in the course.
Enabling License for Demonstrations: To enable students to demonstrate project achievements after the Course concludes (whether operating under Open Science or an alternative track), industry partners agree to make commercially reasonable efforts to grant a three-year limited license for noncommercial use to limited or simulated Partner Data, and/or Partner Code ("Enabling License") minimally required exclusively for the purposes of enabling demonstration for noncommercial use. For example, an AI model trained on Partner Data cannot easily be "un-trained" on that data, but students may wish to demonstrate that model to graduate schools or employers as part of their project under the Enabling License. In this example, demonstrating the trained AI model would be covered under the Enabling License, but reusing, distilling, reverse engineering, extracting, or otherwise disclosing the data used to train the AI model would not be covered. Any data or code in the Enabling License remains subject to nondisclosure for the duration of the Enabling License; a student may demonstrate the functionality of the software, but not disclose the source code of the software without express consent. The Enabling License does not permit commercial use; commercial use of the Limited License may not use any of the "limited or simulated Partner Data, and/or Partner Code" in the Enabling License. To avoid doubt, employment interviews, research discussions, or the like are not "commercial use."
Video Demos: Students may make video "demo" recordings of a working project, redacting any confidential information. They may share these videos in a non-public manner (e.g. on a password-protected website) to illustrate their work in the Course with industry partners' consent, not unreasonably to be withheld.
Publication: Students and industry partners may publish nonconfidential results with the consent of, and with credit to, the other project contributors, and each party agrees not to unreasonably withhold such consent.
Good Faith Negotiation for Alternative Terms: Students and industry partners agree to negotiate in good faith if they wish to agree on other terms regarding Course IP, such as full ownership by the industry partner, patent assignments, etc.
Generative AI and Account Usage: Students agree to use their Harvard accounts and Harvard tools provided for generative AI, described at https://www.huit.harvard.edu/ai/tools, in order to protect students', teammates', and partners' intellectual property. Exception: partners may request students use their own tools, at their own expense, to protect partner IP.
Section 2. Background & Harvard OTD Coursework Policy
In this course, students will work collaboratively with industry partners on applied computation projects. Some of these projects may be suitable for further development and even commercialization. In order to secure resources from industry partners, which may include without limitation their valuable time, data, know-how, and computing resources (some of which partners are obligated to protect) we have adopted the following policy for the course. As such, it may be helpful for students to bear in mind not only Harvard's IP Policy and the SEAS "Intellectual Property Q&A" document (above, the "FAQ"), which has been developed with SEAS students in mind, but also a few additional points that you may wish to consider as students form teams and carry out their coursework, as described below.
Ownership of software and other innovations arising from coursework
In applying the University's IP Policy, Harvard's Office of Technology Development (OTD) has created an informal exception regarding the ownership of innovations made in a student's performance of coursework: provided that the course is not funded by, or conducted under, an agreement between Harvard and a third party (such as a funding agency, research collaborator or provider of technology or data necessary for the course to be offered), and also provided that the only Harvard-administered resources used by a given student are those provided generally to those enrolled in the course, Harvard does not claim ownership of a student's rights in the IP that results from their work. This course is not supported by any agreement between Harvard and any outside party; therefore, your rights in software or other inventions created in the course are governed only by this policy and any other agreements you may make with your classmates and industry partners. (See also #8 of the FAQ.)
On team-based activities, if more than one person authors a piece of software or makes an invention, it will have multiple owners, and each owner's rights must be taken into account if it is to be commercialized. Under our baseline Open Science policy, these issues are resolved upfront by releasing the software under an open license (such as MIT) so that all students, industry partners, and the public have unrestricted freedom to use and develop the work.
Section 3. Course Staff Independence & Firewall
Do course staff have IP rights in our projects?
Course staff, including the teaching assistants, teaching fellows, course assistants, etc. (collectively, "TA's") and instructor, are Harvard employees. Course staff advise and coordinate the project work, but have agreed not to contribute intellectual property to projects. As such, course staff do not claim their work as course staff gives them any IP ownership in or license to your project work. Course staff are neutral academic evaluators and remain completely outside of any private IP or NDA agreements executed between students and industry partners.
Section 4. Legal Principles: Copyright, Patents, and Trade Secrets
What intellectual property (IP) rules apply to the software we create?
Each team member who contributes to a piece of software or determines the method steps that it performs has an interest in it as an author, an inventor or both. Your rights in the IP in terms of allowing or denying its use by others are equal. This does not necessarily mean that each person's financial stake is the same – instead, that is subject to negotiation among the owners. Under the baseline Open Science model, everyone agrees to grant permissive public rights; where projects do not follow the baseline, "owners" include students and industry partners.
(a) Copyright
First, software is copyrightable work. A person who contributes their original material to a piece of software typically would be considered one of its "authors" under copyright law. This could include designs, specifications or code. Although as individual authors you will enjoy certain rights, copyright law prohibits an author of a joint work of authorship from accepting any money or other compensation in exchange for exercising, or allowing someone else to exercise, rights under the copyright unless they account to all of the other authors. In other words, one author must get the explicit consent of all of their co-authors, whether in exchange for a share of the proceeds, a reciprocal grant of consent or other mutually-agreed consideration.
For example, you would not be able to license the work to any third party for compensation or use it in a startup company that you might without the explicit consent of all authors. From this perspective, keeping lines of communication among teammates open (and cordial) as you work together will help you to understand each other's aims and plan together how best to move forward. This is a key reason why the Course adopts Open Science (MIT License) as its baseline: by licensing the software openly from the outset, deadlocks among co-authors are eliminated, and all team members retain the permanent right to use the codebase.
As a working engineer, you should get into the habit of reviewing the terms and conditions of licenses that cover any open-source software or tools, such as generative AI tools, that you might use. (Terms for Claude, ChatGPT, and Gemini are included in further resources below.) In this way, you can make informed decisions about their use, based on the extent to which they might impact your complete ownership, or at least control, of the innovations that you might make through their use.
In the U.S., at least, a work is copyrighted from the moment of its creation; however, you may also wish to consider copyright registration for your code, which can provide additional protections.
(b) Patent
Sometimes, a patent application may be filed to cover the methods carried out by a given software program. Unlike the copyright that may cover software, a patent may, to a limited extent, be commercialized by one of several patent co-owners working alone: the inventor can license their interest in the patent in the U.S., effectively creating a non-exclusive license without enforcement rights, and can themselves practice the patent commercially throughout the world: in other words, be paid for patented products and services that they might manufacture, provide and sell through a start-up that they might found. Such a strategy, of course, has its limitations: non-exclusive licenses are a 'tough sell,' absent the means to enforce the patent, and start-ups that do not hold all of the rights in their foundational IP may find it nearly impossible to attract investment. For these reasons, it is best for team members to run through various scenarios together as they work on their course project and, if any has commercial plans for their course IP, to try to build a business model that takes all inventors' rights into account.
The "attorney of record," i.e., the one who prepares and files the application, is responsible for applying the legal standard of inventorship to those who have contributed to a given invention. As students in this course will work collaboratively in teams, you should expect that the attorney will consider each team member's and partner's contributions in making their legal determination of inventorship.
Assuming that no inventor owes their assignment to Harvard, the patent would be owned by all inventors. Again, Harvard will not claim ownership of your rights in your course-related IP unless you use Harvard-administered resources other than those provided to students generally through the course; however, if any inventor should use other resources or involve anyone in your work who is not a student in the course, it is possible that Harvard, a non-teammate individual or an individual's employer (whether Harvard or another organization) might own their rights, potentially impeding a team's efforts to commercialize its course-related IP. For this reason, it is important for team members to exercise good "hygiene" in terms of both resource use and the involvement of people outside of the team. For example, if you are a graduate student and incorporate into your coursework any research that could be subject to a participation agreement with Harvard, that research may be considered other "Harvard-administered resources". Please consult with the course head and OTD about IP implications around more complex situations.
Finally, a "public disclosure" before the filing of a patent application may affect or invalidate your patent rights in the US or other countries. This may include presentations at meetings, posters, abstracts, print or online publications or announcements of your invention or results generated through its use, pitches to potential investors, competition judges or business partners, etc. In projects governed by open-source baseline rules, publication to a public repository constitutes public disclosure. Where a project is subject to an alternative proprietary track, no student should effect a public disclosure, for example, publish any project materials on a public-facing website that does not require users to log in, or post project code to a public code repository, without the consent of all teammates including the partner. It is anticipated that course-related discussions of team projects will be limited to a private group of course participants, including students, partners, staff, invited guests, and advisors; however, because such individuals are under no obligation of non-disclosure, responsibility for protecting any patentable inventions prior to these discussions rests with you, your teammates, and your partners. Course events, including meetings, presentations, and poster sessions, are private and restricted to course participants and invited guests.
(c) Trade Secret
Trade secret is a form of IP protection that applies only in limited circumstances in which information key to a business is guarded by security measures commensurate in strength and scope with the importance of that information to the company's business interests. Because your work is being created in the context of a university class where other student groups and course staff see and respond to your work, i.e., where there is an expectation of information sharing, and because of the open nature of academic research generally, we do not believe that trade secret protection is likely to apply to any IP that you create in the course.
Section 5. Startups, Commercialization & Licensing Options
What if some of us want to start a startup or join our partner as employees based on our project, but not everyone is going to join?
We strongly urge you to talk about and agree on how your project IP can be used by team members in the future. Under our baseline policy, releasing the project under a permissive open-source license (such as MIT or Apache 2.0) resolves this cleanly: anyone on the team can use the open-source code as the foundation for a startup or future commercial endeavor without needing to buy out former teammates.
If your team has not opted into the baseline Open Science model, remember that in terms of copyrights that cover your software, each co-author has an effective "veto" on commercialization: an agreement among all authors is crucial. In another example, you might agree that any group of one or more authors/inventors could create a startup based on the project IP, in exchange for compensation to the non-participating authors/inventors for their consent, such as cash or an equity stake. In all cases, keeping open communication early in the semester prevents downstream deadlocks. The instructor can help you find appropriate resources to evaluate your options and document your decision, including local patent attorneys who have agreed to hold office hours for interested students, or the Harvard i-Lab, which offers students free support.
